WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:18.000
This is 99% invisible. I'm Roman Mars. There's a big, old apartment building in South Minneapolis that looks out of place. It's in a residential neighborhood with small bungalows and some auto body shops. But this apartment building fills up an entire corner lot.
这里是《99%隐形》。我是罗曼·马尔斯。在南明尼阿波利斯有一栋又大又旧的公寓楼，看起来与周围格格不入。它坐落在一个住宅区，周围是小小的平房和一些汽车修理厂。但这栋公寓楼却占据了整个街角地块。

2
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:36.000
I've bypassed this place hundreds of times, and it always struck me as kind of weird. That's reporter Katie Thornton. I started digging through old newspapers and I found out why it's there. That building is the last remnant of an early 1900s amusement park. It was called Wonderland. A friend of a friend named Hillary lives there now
我经过这里几百次了，它总让我觉得有点奇怪。那是记者凯蒂·桑顿。我开始翻阅旧报纸，终于发现了它存在的原因。那栋建筑是20世纪初一个游乐园的最后遗迹。它叫“仙境”。一个朋友的朋友希拉里现在住在那儿，

3
00:00:36.000 --> 00:01:10.000
and she gave me a tour. We are at my apartment building. It's on the corner of 31st and 31st since South Minneapolis. As I understand it, there was an amusement park here. The park had a roller coaster and a dance hall and a log flume, but the biggest attraction was something much stranger. A bizarre side show that used to fill the apartment complex where Hillary lives now. When she signed the lease, her landlord told her the building used to be called the Infantorium.
她带我参观了一下。我们现在就在我的公寓楼里。它位于南明尼阿波利斯的第31街和第31街的拐角处。据我所知，这里曾经有一个游乐园。公园里有过山车、舞厅和急流勇进，但最大的景点是某种更奇怪的东西。一个离奇的杂耍表演，曾经就填满了希拉里现在居住的这栋公寓楼。当她签租约时，房东告诉她，这栋楼过去叫“婴儿馆”。

4
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:46.000
What he said at the time was like they had premature babies living in this building and being taken care of in the building as part of the attraction of the park. Visitors to the Infantorium would pay 10 cents to enter a spacious room full of glass boxes. They were incubators with tiny premature babies on display. And this wasn't the only place this was happening. The fact that there was a pavilion for incubator babies at Wonderland is not an isolated incident.
他当时说，就像是他们把早产儿养在这栋楼里，作为公园景点的一部分来照顾。参观“婴儿馆”的游客会花10美分进入一个宽敞的房间，里面摆满了玻璃箱。那是些保育箱，里面展示着小小的早产儿。而且这并不是唯一发生这种情况的地方。在“仙境”游乐园里有一个保育箱婴儿展馆，这并非孤立事件。

5
00:01:46.000 --> 00:02:30.000
Lauren McBenefits is an amusement park historian. Incubators for premature babies were oddly enough a phenomenon at the turn of the century that was available at state and county fairs and amusement parks rather than hospitals. At this moment in history, if you wanted your at-risk premature baby to survive, you pretty much had to bring them to an amusement park. These incubator shows cropped up across America. They were the main source of healthcare for premature babies for over 40 years. for premature babies for over 40 years.
劳伦·麦克贝内菲茨是一位游乐园历史学家。奇怪的是，早产儿保育箱在世纪之交是一种现象，它更多出现在州博览会、县博览会和游乐园，而不是医院。在那个历史时刻，如果你想让你处于危险中的早产儿存活下来，你几乎必须把他们带到游乐园去。这些保育箱展览在美国各地涌现。在超过40年的时间里，它们是早产儿医疗保健的主要来源。

6
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:57.000
When the incubator attraction started in the late 1800s, the world was a lot deadlier. Almost one in five babies in the United States died before childhood, and that was an improvement from the previous decades. At the turn of the century, 19 of 20 births were still happening at home, which isn't a problem if your baby is healthy, but not all of them were. Like today, a lot of babies were born too early. The majority of American hospitals had nothing to help them. No technology, no special skills.
当保育箱景点在19世纪末开始出现时，世界要致命得多。在美国，几乎每五个婴儿中就有一个在童年之前夭折，这与过去几十年相比已经是一种进步了。在世纪之交，20个新生儿中仍有19个是在家里出生的，如果你的宝宝很健康，这没问题，但并非所有宝宝都如此。和今天一样，很多婴儿出生得太早。大多数美国医院没有任何东西可以帮助他们。没有技术，也没有特殊技能。

7
00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:26.000
There was no central heating to keep them warm. Doctors would place heated bricks in cribs and cross their fingers. In private, people tried everything to keep premature babies alive. They put these infants and boxes stuffed with feathers to keep them warm. They rubbed oil on the babies and kept them near fireplaces. But more than three-quarters of premature babies were dying. In America, no one in the medical community was trying to solve this problem. But things look different over in France.
当时没有中央供暖来保持他们的体温。医生们会把加热过的砖块放在婴儿床里，然后祈祷好运。在私下里，人们尝试各种方法来让早产儿活下来。他们把这些婴儿放在塞满羽毛的盒子里来保暖。他们给婴儿涂油，并把他们放在壁炉旁。但超过四分之三的早产儿还是夭折了。在美国，医疗界没有人试图解决这个问题。但在法国，情况看起来不同。

8
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:55.000
French doctors stole an idea from the poultry industry, which used incubators to hatch chicken eggs. They designed a human version. Basically, it was a warm box heated by a hot water tank below. In the 1890s, a Frenchman named Alexander Lyon modernized the whole thing. The air in Lyon's incubator was heated by a pipe flowing with hot water. The temperature was pretty consistent, and the box was ventilated. But Lyon's real innovation was to put a big glass window on the box.
法国医生从家禽业偷学了一个点子，家禽业使用孵化器来孵化鸡蛋。他们设计了一个人类版本。基本上，它就是一个由下方热水箱加热的暖箱。在19世纪90年代，一个叫亚历山大·里昂的法国人将整个装置现代化了。里昂保育箱里的空气是由流着热水的管道加热的。温度相当稳定，而且箱子有通风装置。但里昂真正的创新是在箱子上装了一个大玻璃窗。

9
00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:30.000
And then, he filled the box with a baby. He built a better machine, and he displayed it at the Berlin Industrial Exposition in 1896. Don Raffel is the author of the new book about the unexpected origins of modern incubation. He showed the new machine with preemies inside and it was a sensation. There were drinking hall songs about it. He called it Dijkinder Brutenstall which was literally child hatchery and so it took on the environment of a side show. Some carnival showmen bought knockoff machines
然后，他把一个婴儿放进了箱子里。他制造了一台更好的机器，并在1896年的柏林工业博览会上展示了它。唐·拉斐尔是关于现代保育箱意外起源的新书作者。他展示了里面装着早产儿的新机器，这引起了轰动。当时甚至有关于它的酒馆歌曲。他称之为“Dijkinder Brutenstall”，字面意思是“儿童孵化场”，于是它带上了杂耍表演的氛围。一些嘉年华表演者买了仿制机器，

10
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:58.000
and started charging entry to their own premature baby shows. But keeping a baby alive is more complicated than putting them in a box and flipping a switch. So most of them got out of the business pretty quickly. But one showman was hooked on this idea. In 1897, Dr. Martin Cooney put on his first incubator baby show in London. Somehow he may have just gotten down to the idea of this is a kind of a cool way to make money in the beginning.
并开始对他们自己的早产儿展览收费。但让婴儿活下来比把他们放进盒子里并按下开关要复杂得多。所以他们大多数人很快就退出了这个行业。但有一位表演者迷上了这个点子。1897年，马丁·库尼医生在伦敦举办了他的第一场保育箱婴儿展。不知何故，他可能只是想到了这是一种赚钱的好方法。

11
00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:27.000
So he went with this show. Unlike other showmen, Cooney hired nurses to hold the babies and feed them breast milk. The babies were healthier and survived at higher rates. The public loved it. So Dr. Cuny decided to try it out in the United States, at the Omaha World's Fair. Details were always a little vague when Cuny told his life story to Americans, but a few points stayed consistent. He was from Europe, and he was trained in Germany and France,
所以他坚持做了这个展览。与其他表演者不同，库尼雇佣了护士来抱婴儿并喂他们母乳。婴儿们更健康，存活率也更高。公众很喜欢它。于是库尼医生决定在美国的奥马哈世界博览会上尝试一下。当库尼向美国人讲述他的人生故事时，细节总是有点模糊，但有几点是一致的。他来自欧洲，在德国和法国受过培训，

12
00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:49.000
which were decades ahead of the US in terms of medical education. He called himself Dr. Martin Kune, though his given name was actually Michael Kohn. And he said he'd just arrived in the US for the first time, though he'd actually immigrated from Poland about a decade earlier. Like a good showman, he had a knack for exaggeration. Sometimes he said he invented the incubators he didn't.
这两个国家在医学教育方面领先美国几十年。他自称马丁·库尼医生，尽管他的本名其实是迈克尔·科恩。他说他刚第一次来到美国，尽管他其实大约十年前就从波兰移民过来了。像个优秀的表演者一样，他有夸大的天赋。有时他说他发明了保育箱，其实他并没有。

13
00:05:49.000 --> 00:06:19.000
Dr. Cuny beckoned people to his show with bold, hand-painted signs that said things like wonderful invention and infant incubators with living infants. Lots of spectators came and so did parents of premature babies who handed them over to Dr. Cooney, hoping for a miracle. Cooney perfected his side show at the 1901 World's Fair in Buffalo, New York. That fair has gone down in infamy as the place where President Wayne McKenley was shot and later died of gangrene.
库尼医生用大胆的手绘招牌吸引人们来看他的展览，上面写着“奇妙的发明”和“带有活体婴儿的婴儿保育箱”之类的话。很多观众来了，早产儿的父母也来了，他们把孩子交给库尼医生，希望能有奇迹发生。库尼在1901年纽约布法罗的世界博览会上完善了他的杂耍表演。那届博览会因威廉·麦金莱总统在那里被枪击并最终死于坏疽而臭名昭著。

14
00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:41.000
But aside from that, people had a really good time at the fair. Dr. Cooney was set up on the midway. It's the section filled with carnival rides and side shows. And then Buffalo, it was popping off with attractions like House Upside Down and Jerusalem on the morning of the crucifixion. Thousands of people paid 10 cents each to see Dr. Cuny's incubator show.
但除此之外，人们在博览会上玩得很开心。库尼医生在游乐场中段安营扎寨。那是充满嘉年华游乐设施和杂耍表演的区域。在布法罗，那里有“颠倒屋”和“受难日的耶路撒冷”等景点，非常火爆。成千上万的人每人花10美分去看库尼医生的保育箱展览。

15
00:06:41.000 --> 00:07:09.000
They had barkers outside saying, don't forget to see the babies. Maybe the future president is inside. He knew how to be a showman. He knew how to talk to people, and he loved talking to people. Dr. Cuny greeted thousands of people on the midway. His exhibit was full of futuristic glass incubators. One reporter wrote in Cosmopolitan that Kuni's incubator babies were more exciting than Niagara Falls.
他们在外面有招揽客人的吆喝者说：“别忘了看婴儿。也许未来的总统就在里面。”他知道如何成为一名表演者。他知道如何与人交谈，而且他热爱与人交谈。库尼医生在游乐场中段迎接了成千上万的人。他的展位里摆满了未来主义的玻璃保育箱。一位记者在《大都会》杂志上写道，库尼的保育箱婴儿比尼亚加拉大瀑布更令人兴奋。

16
00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:32.000
Newspapers as far away as Hawaii reported on the survival of the infants. It was like a reality TV show in some ways where there were people who would come every week to look at the babies and they'd have a favorite that they'd be rooting for. A local medical journal reported that 48 of the 52 babies delivered to Cuny that summer had survived.
远至夏威夷的报纸都报道了这些婴儿的存活情况。在某种程度上，这就像一个真人秀节目，有些人每周都会来看这些婴儿，他们会有自己支持的“心头好”。当地一份医学杂志报道称，那个夏天送到库尼那里的52个婴儿中有48个存活了下来。

17
00:07:32.000 --> 00:08:18.000
But even so, American doctors were not banging down Cuny's door for his machines. This life-saving technology was stuck on the midway. Despite the big crowds and the good press, Cuny was still spending more money than he was bringing in. midway. basically had to build a NICU. And then when the whole thing is over, you have to tear it all down. Luckily for CUNY, a new fad was sweeping the country in the early 1900s. Amusement parks took all the excitement of the fair midways and made them permanent.
但即便如此，美国医生并没有为了他的机器而踏破库尼的门槛。这项救命的技术被困在了游乐场中段。尽管观众众多，媒体评价也不错，但库尼的支出仍然超过了收入。基本上必须建造一个新生儿重症监护室。然后当一切结束时，你必须把它全部拆掉。幸运的是，20世纪初，一种新风尚席卷了全国。游乐园把博览会中段的所有兴奋点都变成了永久性的。

18
00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:43.000
In the early 1900s, amusement parks were popping up all over the country, like all over the country. In 1909, a reporter from Pittsburgh apologized on behalf of the city. He wrote, we regret that we are possibly the only city in the country of any consequence that will go through the coming summer season with only three amusement parks. By 1910, every US city with at least 20,000 people had their own park.
在20世纪初，游乐园在全国各地如雨后春笋般涌现。1909年，一位来自匹兹堡的记者代表该市道歉。他写道：“我们很遗憾，我们可能是全国唯一一个在即将到来的夏季只有三个游乐园的重要城市。”到1910年，每个拥有至少2万人口的美国城市都有了自己的公园。

19
00:08:43.000 --> 00:09:10.000
People wanted to have fun, and amusement parks were all about unbridled pleasure. It's not an intellectual thing at all, it's just a kind of giddy physical amusement. The attendance figures for these parks are phenomenal. You could get things like a quarter of the population of a medium-sized city on a weekend. Most of the early parks were owned by electric trolley companies who use the parks to get passengers on their trains.
人们想要娱乐，而游乐园就是关于肆无忌惮的快乐。这根本不是什么理性的东西，只是一种令人眩晕的身体娱乐。这些公园的入场人数非常惊人。周末时，你甚至能吸引到一个中等城市四分之一的人口。大多数早期的公园都由电力电车公司拥有，他们利用公园来吸引乘客乘坐他们的火车。

20
00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:33.000
The trolley lines would also end at the amusement park and the electric cables from the train would be hooked up to power the rollercoasters. The parks were places where new technology was smuggled into American society under the guise of entertainment. At the time, there was a lot of uneasiness about new technology. Electric trains, lights, and motors weren't always safe, but in amusement parks, technology
电车线路也会在游乐园终点站，火车的电缆会被连接起来为过山车供电。这些公园是新技术以娱乐为幌子被引入美国社会的地方。当时，人们对新技术有很多不安。电动火车、灯光和电机并不总是安全的，但在游乐园里，技术

21
00:09:33.000 --> 00:10:05.000
wasn't something to be feared or mistrusted. Playing with these things, making them part of leisure and recreation, acclimated people to them physically with their bodies, but also made them pleasurable, made them pleasurable at a time when there might be some anxiety about these new technologies. When people got on thrill rides or watched firework displays, they saw that technology could be fun and safe. In a way, Dr. Kuni's incubator babies were the essence of amusement parks.
并不是什么值得恐惧或怀疑的东西。玩这些东西，把它们作为休闲和娱乐的一部分，不仅在身体上让人们适应了它们，而且在人们对这些新技术可能感到焦虑的时候，让它们变得令人愉悦。当人们乘坐惊险游乐设施或观看烟花表演时，他们看到技术可以是既有趣又安全的。在某种程度上，库尼医生的保育箱婴儿就是游乐园的精髓。

22
00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:31.000
And Kuni's exhibit in Buffalo caught the eye of two businessmen who were planning to build a brand new park in Kony Island. They saw this is a really cool show. It's as interesting as a ride. This would work at an amusement park. Kony Island already had two other self-contained amusement parks, and until it burned down in 1896, a seven-story hotel shaped like an elephant. But these businessmen were taking it to the next level.
库尼在布法罗的展览引起了两位计划在康尼岛建造一个全新公园的商人的注意。他们认为这是一个非常酷的表演。它和游乐设施一样有趣。这在游乐园里会很受欢迎。康尼岛已经有两个独立的游乐园，在1896年烧毁之前，还有一个七层楼高的大象形状的酒店。但这些商人要把事情提升到一个新的水平。

23
00:10:31.000 --> 00:11:21.000
Their new endeavor would be called Luna Park. It featured a ride imported from Buffalo, called a trip to the moon. It brought passengers into a paper-machae fantasy world full of scientifically incorrect foliage and 200 actors playing moon people. And among Luna Park's flagship attractions was Dr. Kuni's incubator babies. When the sun set on May 16th, 1903, Luna Park switched on 250,000 electric light bulbs and opened their gates to 60,000 people who gathered outside. Many of them made a beeline for the babies. Coney Island was the start of something big for Dr. Coney. In the spring of 1905, he traveled to Chicago, Denver, and Minneapolis to set up new exhibits.
他们的新事业被称为“月神公园”。它以从布法罗引进的名为“月球之旅”的游乐设施为特色。它把乘客带入一个充满科学上不正确的植物和200名扮演月球人的演员的纸糊幻想世界。月神公园的旗舰景点之一就是库尼医生的保育箱婴儿。1903年5月16日太阳落山时，月神公园点亮了25万个电灯泡，向聚集在外面的6万人敞开了大门。他们中的许多人直奔婴儿展区。康尼岛是库尼医生事业的一个重大开端。1905年春天，他前往芝加哥、丹佛和明尼阿波利斯设立了新的展览。

24
00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:49.000
His incubators captivated audiences across the country and he became stinking rich. Kuni bought a nice house near the shore on Kuni Island where he was known to host extravagant dinners. If Dr. Kuni was in it for the money, he got what he wanted. But the thing is, he made a lot of choices that weren't driven by profit. He really wanted to save babies and to get the incubators into hospitals. In his travels, Kuni would whine and dine doctors and give them demonstrations of the incubators.
他的保育箱吸引了全国各地的观众，他变得非常有钱。库尼在康尼岛岸边买了一栋漂亮的房子，他以举办奢华的晚宴而闻名。如果库尼医生是为了钱，他得到了他想要的。但问题是，他做了很多并非出于利润驱动的选择。他真心想拯救婴儿，并把保育箱引入医院。在旅行中，库尼会宴请医生，并向他们演示保育箱。

25
00:11:49.000 --> 00:12:20.000
On multiple occasions, he tried to get health departments to use his incubators after the local fair season was over. He even tried to donate his incubators to the city of San Francisco, but no one would take them. Doctors had all sorts of reasons for rejecting the technology. One reason was the disgraceful influence of the eugenics movement. In 1901, an anonymous editorial made the rounds in medical journals asking if the incubators should be shut down. The author wrote that the human race suffered by keeping alive babies
在多次场合，他试图在当地博览会季节结束后让卫生部门使用他的保育箱。他甚至试图把他的保育箱捐给旧金山市，但没有人愿意接受。医生们拒绝这项技术有各种各样的理由。其中一个原因是优生学运动的恶劣影响。1901年，一篇匿名社论在医学期刊上流传，询问是否应该关闭这些保育箱。作者写道，人类种族因维持那些

26
